(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["mth-12-04"] = {"mth-12-04-m01":{"idea":"A second-degree equation with no linear or constant term is a pair of lines through the origin.","steps":["Every term has degree two: ax², 2hxy and by².","Divide by x² and put m = y/x: b m² + 2h m + a = 0.","When h² ≥ ab this has real roots m₁ and m₂.","Each root is the slope of a line through the origin."],"others":"A circle needs x² and y² with equal coefficients and a constant. A single line through a point needs linear terms. A parabola needs a squared term in one variable only.","trap":"No terms of lower degree means the lines pass through the origin.","answer":"a pair of straight lines through the origin"},"mth-12-04-m02":{"idea":"Homogeneous means every term has the same degree.","steps":["x², xy and y² each have total degree 2.","So an equation with only such terms is homogeneous of the second degree.","The general second-degree equation also has linear terms and a constant."],"others":"The general second degree equation includes lower-degree terms. A linear equation has degree one. A circle in standard form has a constant term.","trap":"Same degree in every term.","answer":"a homogeneous equation of the second degree"},"mth-12-04-m03":{"idea":"Multiply the equations of the two lines, each written equal to zero.","steps":["y = 2x becomes y − 2x = 0, and y = 3x becomes y − 3x = 0.","(y − 2x)(y − 3x) = y² − 5xy + 6x².","So the pair is 6x² − 5xy + y² = 0."],"others":"x² − 5xy + 6y² = 0 swaps x and y, which gives lines x = 2y and x = 3y. The two with +5xy have the wrong sign.","trap":"Check: slopes are the roots of m² − 5m + 6 = 0.","answer":"6x² − 5xy + y² = 0"},"mth-12-04-m04":{"idea":"The axes are x = 0 and y = 0, so the product is zero.","steps":["The y-axis is x = 0 and the x-axis is y = 0.","xy = 0 holds when x = 0 or y = 0.","So xy = 0 is the combined equation."],"others":"x + y = 0 is one line. x² − y² = 0 gives y = x and y = −x. x² + y² = 0 gives only the origin.","trap":"Product of the two line equations.","answer":"xy = 0"},"mth-12-04-m05":{"idea":"Put y = mx to get a quadratic in m, then use the sum of roots.","steps":["Put y = mx in ax² + 2hxy + by² = 0 to get a + 2hm + bm² = 0.","Sum of roots = −(coefficient of m)/(coefficient of m²).","So m₁ + m₂ = −2h/b."],"others":"−2h/a uses the wrong denominator. 2h/b has the wrong sign. −h/b misses the factor 2.","trap":"The coefficient of m² is b, not a.","answer":"−2h/b"},"mth-12-04-m06":{"idea":"The product of the roots is the constant term over the coefficient of m².","steps":["The quadratic is bm² + 2hm + a = 0.","Product of roots = a/b.","So m₁m₂ = a/b."],"others":"b/a is upside down. 2h/b is the negative of the sum. −a/b has an extra minus sign.","trap":"Constant term a over leading coefficient b.","answer":"a/b"},"mth-12-04-m07":{"idea":"Perpendicular lines have slopes with product −1.","steps":["The product of the slopes is a/b.","For perpendicular lines a/b = −1.","So a = −b, which means a + b = 0."],"others":"h² = ab means the lines coincide. h = 0 does not force perpendicularity. a = b gives a product of 1.","trap":"Perpendicular: a + b = 0.","answer":"a + b = 0"},"mth-12-04-m08":{"idea":"Coincident lines have equal slopes, so the discriminant is zero.","steps":["The slopes come from bm² + 2hm + a = 0.","The discriminant is 4h² − 4ab = 4(h² − ab).","Equal roots need h² = ab, and the equation is a perfect square."],"others":"h = 0 gives lines at equal angles but different slopes. h² > ab gives two distinct lines. a + b = 0 gives perpendicular lines.","trap":"Coincident: h² = ab.","answer":"h² = ab"},"mth-12-04-m09":{"idea":"A sum of two squares is zero only at the origin.","steps":["x² + y² = 0 needs both x² and y² to be zero.","So the only real point is (0, 0).","Here h² − ab = −1, which is negative, so there are no real lines."],"others":"The axes would be xy = 0. A circle of radius 1 would have −1 on the right side. Perpendicular real lines would need a + b = 0, but here a + b = 2.","trap":"No real lines: just the origin.","answer":"only the origin, since there are no real lines"},"mth-12-04-m10":{"idea":"A difference of squares factorises into two lines.","steps":["x² − y² = (x − y)(x + y) = 0.","So x − y = 0 or x + y = 0.","These are y = x and y = −x, which are perpendicular (a + b = 0)."],"others":"The line x + y = 0 alone misses half. The axes would be xy = 0. The line y = x alone misses the other line.","trap":"Factorise first.","answer":"the lines y = x and y = −x"},"mth-12-04-m11":{"idea":"The product of the slopes is a/b.","steps":["For 2x² + 5xy + 2y², a = 2 and b = 2.","Product = a/b = 2/2 = 1.","The sum is −2h/b = −5/2, so the slopes are −2 and −1/2."],"others":"5/2 is h-related. −1 would mean perpendicular, which needs a + b = 0. 2 is just a.","trap":"The product is not −1, so the lines are not perpendicular.","answer":"1"},"mth-12-04-m12":{"idea":"Use tan θ = |2√(h² − ab)/(a + b)|.","steps":["Here a = 1, 2h = −3 and b = 2, so h² − ab = 9/4 − 2 = 1/4.","tan θ = |2 × (1/2)/(1 + 2)| = 1/3.","So the acute angle is tan⁻¹(1/3)."],"others":"tan⁻¹ 3 is the complement. π/4 would need tan θ = 1. tan⁻¹(1/2) comes from a slope, not the angle between the lines.","trap":"Check: slopes 1 and 1/2 give tan θ = 1/3.","answer":"tan⁻¹(1/3)"},"mth-12-04-m13":{"idea":"The general second-degree equation is a pair of lines when a certain determinant is zero.","steps":["The condition is abc + 2fgh − af² − bg² − ch² = 0.","It is the vanishing of the 3 × 3 determinant of the coefficients.","For real lines we also need h² ≥ ab."],"others":"Wrong signs on the terms give a different expression. a + b = 0 only is for perpendicular lines. h² − ab = 0 only is for coincident homogeneous lines.","trap":"Plus 2fgh, minus the other three.","answer":"abc + 2fgh − af² − bg² − ch² = 0"},"mth-12-04-m14":{"idea":"Factorise the quadratic to get the lines.","steps":["x² − 5xy + 6y² = (x − 2y)(x − 3y).","So the lines are x = 2y and x = 3y.","Their slopes are 1/2 and 1/3, not 2 and 3."],"others":"x + 2y = 0 and x + 3y = 0 change the sign. y = 2x and y = 3x swap x and y. x = −2y and x = −3y also change the sign.","trap":"x = 2y means y = x/2.","answer":"x = 2y and x = 3y"},"mth-12-04-m15":{"idea":"Check the sum a + b.","steps":["For 3x² + kxy − 3y², a + b = 3 − 3 = 0 for any k.","So the lines are perpendicular.","Also h² − ab = k²/4 + 9 is always positive, so the lines are real for every k."],"others":"k = 0 or k = 3 are only particular values. 'For no k' is false, because a + b = 0 always.","trap":"Check reality: h² − ab must be positive.","answer":"for every real value of k"},"mth-12-04-m16":{"idea":"A perfect square gives two coincident lines.","steps":["x² − 6xy + 9y² = (x − 3y)².","Here h = −3, so h² = 9 = ab.","Both lines are x = 3y."],"others":"Perpendicular needs a + b = 0, but a + b = 10. Parallel lines would have a constant term. No real lines would need h² < ab.","trap":"h² = ab means coincident.","answer":"two coincident lines, each x = 3y"},"mth-12-04-m17":{"idea":"Perpendicular lines through the origin need a + b = 0.","steps":["For 2x² + 3xy − 2y² = 0: a + b = 2 + (−2) = 0.","For the others, a + b is 3, 4 and 2, not zero.","So the first equation represents perpendicular lines."],"others":"x² + 3xy + 2y², 3x² + xy + y² and x² − 3xy + y² have a + b equal to 3, 4 and 2.","trap":"Add the coefficients of x² and y².","answer":"2x² + 3xy − 2y² = 0"},"mth-12-04-m18":{"idea":"Both lines pass through the origin, so they meet there.","steps":["The equation has no terms of degree less than two.","(0, 0) satisfies it.","So both lines pass through the origin, and that is their intersection."],"others":"(h, h) and (a, b) are not on the lines in general. A point on the x-axis is only the origin if the lines are the axes.","trap":"Homogeneous means through the origin.","answer":"the origin"},"mth-12-04-m19":{"idea":"The two lines meet where both partial derivatives vanish.","steps":["Differentiate the expression with respect to x and y and set both to zero.","This gives ax + hy + g = 0 and hx + by + f = 0.","Solve these two linear equations to find the point."],"others":"ax + hy = 0 and hx + by = 0 leave out g and f, so they give the origin. The other two options are not derivatives.","trap":"Partial derivatives give the meeting point.","answer":"ax + hy + g = 0 and hx + by + f = 0"},"mth-12-04-m20":{"idea":"Complete the square to see the two lines.","steps":["x² + 2x + 1 = (x + 1)².","So the equation is (x + 1)² − y² = 0.","That factorises as (x + 1 − y)(x + 1 + y) = 0.","The lines y = x + 1 and y = −(x + 1) are perpendicular and meet at (−1, 0)."],"others":"A hyperbola has a non-zero constant on the right side. A circle would have x² + y². Parallel lines would have equal slopes.","trap":"Look for a perfect square.","answer":"two perpendicular lines that meet at (−1, 0)"},"mth-12-04-m21":{"idea":"Multiply the equations of the two lines.","steps":["Write them as x + y − 1 = 0 and x − y − 1 = 0.","(x + y − 1)(x − y − 1) = (x − 1)² − y².","= x² − 2x + 1 − y², so x² − y² − 2x + 1 = 0."],"others":"x² − y² + 2x + 1 = 0 has the wrong sign on 2x. x² + y² − 2x + 1 = 0 has +y². x² − y² − 1 = 0 misses the 2x terms.","trap":"Watch the sign of the linear term.","answer":"x² − y² − 2x + 1 = 0"},"mth-12-04-m22":{"idea":"Each line through the origin with slope m is y − mx = 0.","steps":["The two lines are y − mx = 0 and y − nx = 0.","Their combined equation is the product (y − mx)(y − nx) = 0.","Expanding: y² − (m + n)xy + mn x² = 0."],"others":"y² − (m + n)xy = 0 leaves out the mn x² term. (x − my)(x − ny) has slopes 1/m and 1/n. The plus signs give slopes −m and −n.","trap":"y minus slope times x.","answer":"(y − mx)(y − nx) = 0"},"mth-12-04-m23":{"idea":"A zero denominator in tan θ means θ = 90°.","steps":["tan θ = |2√(h² − ab)/(a + b)|.","If a + b = 0, the denominator is zero, so tan θ is infinite.","Then θ = 90°, and the lines are perpendicular."],"others":"45° and 0° do not give an infinite tangent. 'Not defined, lines do not exist' is wrong: the lines exist.","trap":"a + b = 0 means perpendicular.","answer":"90°, as the lines are perpendicular"},"mth-12-04-m24":{"idea":"A negative discriminant means no real slopes.","steps":["bm² + 2hm + a = 0 has discriminant 4(h² − ab).","If h² < ab, the discriminant is negative.","So there are no real slopes, and the only real point is the origin."],"others":"Real distinct lines need h² > ab. Parallel lines do not pass through the origin here. Coincident lines need h² = ab.","trap":"Example: x² + xy + y² = 0.","answer":"only the origin, since no real lines exist"},"mth-12-04-m25":{"idea":"Put y = mx and solve for the slopes.","steps":["6x² − 5xy + y² = 0 gives 6 − 5m + m² = 0.","(m − 2)(m − 3) = 0, so m = 2 or 3.","Check: the sum is 5 = −2h/b and the product is 6 = a/b."],"others":"6 and 1 are coefficients, not slopes. 1/2 and 1/3 come from swapping x and y. −2 and −3 have the wrong sign.","trap":"Put y = mx, not x = my.","answer":"2 and 3"},"mth-12-04-m26":{"idea":"Real and distinct lines need a positive discriminant.","steps":["The discriminant of bm² + 2hm + a = 0 is 4h² − 4ab.","It is positive when h² > ab.","Then there are two different real slopes."],"others":"h² = ab gives coincident lines. h² < ab gives no real lines. a + b = 0 only means perpendicular, if the lines are real.","trap":"h² > ab for two real different lines.","answer":"h² > ab"},"mth-12-04-n01":{"idea":"Sum of the slopes is −2h/b.","steps":["2h = 7, so h = 7/2, and b = 3.","m₁ + m₂ = −2h/b = −7/3.","= −2.333."],"trap":"Check: (2x + y)(x + 3y) has slopes −2 and −1/3, which sum to −7/3.","answer":"-2.333"},"mth-12-04-n02":{"idea":"Put y = mx and solve the quadratic in m.","steps":["6x² − 5xy + y² = 0 gives 6 − 5m + m² = 0.","(m − 2)(m − 3) = 0, so m = 2 or 3.","The larger slope is 3."],"trap":"Factorised form: (y − 2x)(y − 3x) = 0.","answer":"3"},"mth-12-04-n03":{"idea":"Use tan θ = |2√(h² − ab)/(a + b)|.","steps":["a = 1, h = −3/2, b = 2, so h² − ab = 9/4 − 2 = 1/4.","tan θ = |2 × (1/2)/3| = 1/3.","θ = tan⁻¹(1/3) = 18.43°."],"trap":"Check: lines x = y and x = 2y have slopes 1 and 1/2: 45° − 26.57° = 18.43°.","answer":"18.43°"},"mth-12-04-n04":{"idea":"Use the angle formula with a = b = 3 and h = 5.","steps":["h² − ab = 25 − 9 = 16, so √ = 4.","tan θ = |2 × 4/(3 + 3)| = 8/6 = 4/3.","θ = tan⁻¹(4/3) = 53.13°."],"trap":"2h = 10, so h = 5.","answer":"53.13°"},"mth-12-04-n05":{"idea":"Perpendicular lines need a + b = 0.","steps":["For kx² + 4xy − y² = 0, a = k and b = −1.","a + b = k − 1 = 0.","So k = 1."],"trap":"Check: the product of slopes a/b = k/(−1) = −1 gives k = 1.","answer":"1"},"mth-12-04-n06":{"idea":"Use the condition for a pair of lines.","steps":["a = 1, b = −1, h = 0, g = 1, f = 0 and c = k.","abc + 2fgh − af² − bg² − ch² = −k + 0 − 0 + 1 − 0 = 0.","So k = 1."],"trap":"Check: x² + 2x + 1 − y² = (x + 1)² − y², which factorises into two lines.","answer":"1"},"mth-12-04-n07":{"idea":"Complete the squares to find the two lines and their meeting point.","steps":["x² − 2x + 1 − (y² − 4y + 4) = x² − y² − 2x + 4y − 3.","So the equation is (x − 1)² − (y − 2)² = 0.","The lines are y − 2 = ±(x − 1), and they meet at (1, 2).","The sum of the coordinates is 1 + 2 = 3."],"trap":"The meeting point is where both brackets are zero.","answer":"3"},"mth-12-04-n08":{"idea":"Find where the two lines meet the line x = 4.","steps":["x² − 4y² = (x − 2y)(x + 2y) = 0 gives y = x/2 and y = −x/2.","At x = 4 they meet at (4, 2) and (4, −2).","The base on x = 4 has length 4 and the height is 4 (from the origin).","Area = ½ × 4 × 4 = 8."],"trap":"The two lines meet at the origin.","answer":"8"},"mth-12-04-n09":{"idea":"Use the sum and product of the slopes to find (m₁ − m₂)².","steps":["m₁ + m₂ = −2h/b = −5/2 and m₁m₂ = a/b = 1.","(m₁ − m₂)² = (m₁ + m₂)² − 4m₁m₂.","= 25/4 − 4 = 9/4 = 2.25."],"trap":"Check: slopes −2 and −1/2 differ by 1.5, and 1.5² = 2.25.","answer":"2.25"},"mth-12-04-n10":{"idea":"A pair of real lines through the origin needs h² ≥ ab.","steps":["x² − 4y² = 0: h = 0, ab = −4, so h² − ab = 4 > 0: yes.","x² + y² = 0: h² − ab = −1 < 0: no.","2x² + 3xy + y² = 0: 9/4 − 2 = 1/4 > 0: yes.","x² + xy + y² = 0: 1/4 − 1 < 0: no. So 2 of the 4."],"trap":"Check the sign of h² − ab for each.","answer":"2"}};
